Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:34:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] macos: the guest's caches are the host's, read from XNU and
 Hypervisor.framework

Firecracker gives the guest the host's caches in its device tree, which
it reads in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cache (cache_info.rs). macOS has
no sysfs: Firecracker logged "No L1 caches found in sysfs" and the guest
had no cache entries ("cacheinfo: Unable to detect cache hierarchy for
CPU 0").

On macOS readln_special reads each file from wick's ext::sysfs instead
(overlay/vmm/ext/sysfs.rs), in sysfs's format: the leaves of the vCPU's
CLIDR_EL1, sizes from XNU's hw.l1dcachesize, hw.l1icachesize and
hw.l2cachesize, the number of sets and the line size from the vCPU's
CCSIDR_EL1 (hv_vcpu_config_get_ccsidr_el1_sys_reg_values; no sysctl
gives them), checked against XNU's size, and the CPUs sharing a level
from hw.cacheconfig. Linux's code path is unchanged.

Upstream test it unlocks: test_cache_topology with 1 and 2 vCPUs, PCI on
and off (macOS 27.2, M1 Pro: L1 data 64K, 128 sets, 64-byte lines; L1
instruction 128K, 256 sets; L2 4096K, 2048 sets, 128-byte lines, shared
by cpu0 and cpu1, as the host's Efficiency cluster).

Still different:
- The caches are those of the lowest performance level, which XNU's
  top-level hw.* report (bsd/kern/kern_mib.c sysctl_hw_generic) and whose
  geometry Hypervisor.framework gives every vCPU; Linux reads cpu0's. On
  the M1 Pro cpu0 is of that level (IODeviceTree cluster-type "E").
- With 16 vCPUs the test still fails: the M1 Pro has 10 CPUs, and macOS
  gives no geometry for a Performance core's caches.
- override_clidr cannot change the guest's CLIDR_EL1: Hypervisor.framework
  has no register for it (hv_vcpu_types.h), so its read fails and it warns
  and leaves it, as upstream does on a KVM that cannot read it. The guest
  reads the hardware's (0x81000023 on the M1 Pro: L1 separate, L2 unified,
  as the device tree says; LoC 1, where KVM from 6.10 would write 2).
